WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:01.969
From far away, Earth looks calm:

00:00:02.019 --> 00:00:04.848
a blue and white marble turning

00:00:04.898 --> 00:00:06.266
quietly in the dark.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:08.677
But up close, it never sits still.

00:00:08.727 --> 00:00:11.727
For 4.6 billion years, it has been boiling,

00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:14.250
freezing and reshaping itself.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.620
Welcome to the wonders of planet Earth.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.240
Our journey has seventeen stops:

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.440
from rocks falling out of space

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.070
to the deepest ocean,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.331
the wildest winds and the driest deserts.

00:00:30.781 --> 00:00:33.901
Every day, lumps of rock and metal

00:00:33.951 --> 00:00:36.688
from space slam into our atmosphere.

00:00:36.738 --> 00:00:39.451
Friction makes them glow,

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:41.421
and we see them as meteors,

00:00:41.471 --> 00:00:44.262
shooting stars, or blazing fireballs.

00:00:45.012 --> 00:00:47.482
In November 1833, the most brilliant

00:00:47.532 --> 00:00:50.178
meteor shower ever recorded lit up

00:00:50.228 --> 00:00:52.932
the skies over the United States.

00:00:52.982 --> 00:00:54.797
Many people woke up convinced

00:00:54.847 --> 00:00:56.362
the world was ending.

